TITLE:Rec Dec Mini-games
H1:Rec dec mini-games: the wall, the pool, the kayaks <span class="draft">plan v1 · plan only</span>
SUB:The user (2026-10-19): "each sport (other than floating) should be a little mini game, that's the goal. Design a game / digital toy for the rock wall, the pool and the kayak." Lightdisc already is one. This page offers two ideas for each of the three with my pick, all built the way Lightdisc is. Written by the Pillar 5 designer for lead 16. No game code.
NAV:same=Shared rules|wall=Climbing wall|pool=Pool|kayak=Kayaks|trad=Traditions|perf=Building it|q=Questions

<section id="same">
  <h2>What all three share (the Lightdisc way)</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><b>One thumb, short goes:</b> 30 seconds to 3 minutes a go. You only steer or time; the rest is automatic.</li>
    <li><b>The live 3D room:</b> tap the wall, a lane or the kayak dock, walk there, and the camera eases into the game view over about 0.8 s (Lightdisc's dolly-zoom). The near walls fade as the cutaway already does. At the end a result card (Again, Done) and the camera eases back.</li>
    <li><b>The clock stops</b> for the go and its result, as Lightdisc and Tiles do (a <code>PAUSE</code> flag each).</li>
    <li><b>No failing:</b> nobody falls badly, sinks or capsizes for good. A fall ends the go gently and keeps what you managed.</li>
    <li><b>Crew alongside:</b> crew who are free can join (Lightdisc's way of finding someone). They're played by the same rules with a seeded autopilot, and off-screen their result is worked out on paper, so traditions (the regatta) need no drawing.</li>
    <li><b>Rewards:</b> leisure and fitness (as Lightdisc: fitness up, energy a little down), hygiene for the pool, and a friendship step with whoever went with you. Personal bests are kept and said in words ("your best climb yet").</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section id="wall">
  <h2>Climbing wall</h2>
  <div class="tablewrap"><table>
    <tr><th></th><th>★ A. Reach</th><th>B. Bell rush</th></tr>
    <tr><td><b>The toy</b></td><td>Climb hold to hold up one of the four routes to the bell. Your free hand follows your thumb: drag to a glowing hold inside your reach circle and lift to grab it; feet follow by themselves. A grip bar drains on small holds and refills when you hang still on a big one. Ring the bell at the top.</td><td>Timing only: a marker swings over the next hold; tap when it's in the green and you make the move. Three misses and you're lowered.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><b>What you see</b></td><td colspan="2">The live wall at the river hall's stern, the camera behind and a little below you, looking up; the route's holds glow in its colour; the bell at the top of the screen.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><b>A go</b></td><td>1 to 3 minutes (about 12 to 25 moves)</td><td>about 1 minute</td></tr>
    <tr><td><b>Falling</b></td><td colspan="2">Grip runs out (or three misses): the auto-belay lowers you slowly, and the height you reached counts.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><b>Progress</b></td><td>Four routes by colour, easy to hard (holds get smaller and further apart). Topping one rings the bell and puts your name on the season's bell board. Best height per route.</td><td>One route that speeds up.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><b>Crew</b></td><td colspan="2">The wall has two auto-belays: a friend climbs the other route beside you (Tomas on Wall night). "Race me?" turns it into a race to the bell.</td></tr>
  </table></div>
  <p class="note">Why Reach: it feels like climbing (choosing your next hold), and the four coloured routes give it somewhere to go over weeks. Bell rush is quicker to build but a plain timing game.</p>
</section>

<section id="pool">
  <h2>Pool</h2>
  <div class="tablewrap"><table>
    <tr><th></th><th>★ A. Lengths</th><th>B. Ring dive</th></tr>
    <tr><td><b>The toy</b></td><td>Swim four lengths in rhythm. A ring closes in on your swimmer; tap anywhere as it meets the beat for a clean stroke (good timing = smooth speed, mistimed = a splash and a slower stroke). Every few strokes, hold your thumb down to breathe. At the wall, swipe up for a tumble turn.</td><td>Dive from the side and steer underwater with your thumb (where your thumb is, you go) to pick up rings on the Wren mosaic before your breath runs out; come up, breathe, dive again.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><b>What you see</b></td><td>The live pool, the camera above and behind your lane, looking down it towards the hull windows and the stars; the lane ropes either side.</td><td>Above the water from the side, then under it: the mosaic and caustic light (a new underwater look).</td></tr>
    <tr><td><b>A go</b></td><td>about 45 to 70 seconds (four short lengths)</td><td>1 to 2 minutes (three dives)</td></tr>
    <tr><td><b>Progress</b></td><td>Your best four lengths, said in words; smoother rhythm over weeks unlocks the faster stroke (front crawl after breaststroke).</td><td>Most rings in three dives.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><b>Crew</b></td><td colspan="2">Four lanes, one swimmer each: up to three crew swim beside you. "Race me?" makes it a race; otherwise they keep their own pace.</td></tr>
  </table></div>
  <p class="note">Why Lengths: it's what a pool is for, it fits Dawn swim exactly, and it stays above the water (no new underwater look to build). Ring dive was dropped as a tradition (you said neither), but it could still come later as a toy.</p>
</section>

<section id="kayak">
  <h2>Kayaks</h2>
  <div class="tablewrap"><table>
    <tr><th></th><th>★ A. White water</th><th>B. Duck rescue</th></tr>
    <tr><td><b>The toy</b></td><td>One lap of the loop: the slow channel, then the fast white-water run along the windows. Your kayak heads where your thumb is (Lightdisc's steering); tap for a strong stroke, with a short rest between. Pass through the slalom gates hung over the fast run (red and green poles); a missed gate adds two seconds. The wave gate makes standing waves that push you about.</td><td>Pell's rubber ducks are loose in the river. Paddle the slow loop scooping them up (steer with your thumb); no clock, a calm toy.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><b>What you see</b></td><td>The live river hall, the camera following behind your kayak (a chase view), the next gate ahead.</td><td>The same view, slower.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><b>A go</b></td><td>about 60 to 90 seconds (one lap)</td><td>1 to 3 minutes</td></tr>
    <tr><td><b>Progress</b></td><td>The slow loop first; Holt opens the fast run after three Paddle club visits (as agreed). Best lap in words; more gates hung as you get better.</td><td>Ducks found.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><b>Crew</b></td><td colspan="2">Up to three crew paddle with you. At the regatta, heats of four race the lap live.</td></tr>
  </table></div>
  <p class="note">Why White water: it carries two traditions (Paddle club and the regatta), and its steering is Lightdisc's, so it reuses the most. Duck rescue is a sweet idea for a quiet evening and could be its calm "slow loop" mode.</p>
</section>

<section id="trad">
  <h2>How they tie to the traditions</h2>
  <div class="tablewrap"><table>
    <tr><th>Tradition</th><th>The mini-game in it</th></tr>
    <tr><td>Wall night (Tue)</td><td>Tomas climbs the other belay beside you; topping a route that night puts you on the bell board.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Dawn swim (Tue, Thu)</td><td>Rina swims the next lane at her pace: keep with her for her friendship step (not a race).</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Paddle club (Wed)</td><td>Holt's lessons: the slow loop, then the fast run once he says you're ready (three visits).</td></tr>
    <tr><td>River regatta (every 4 weeks)</td><td>Heats of four, live; the others' heats on paper; you can enter a heat, not only the fun heat. The winner's pennant goes up over the dock.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Lightdisc Cup, Winner Stays On</td><td>Already Lightdisc.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Float hour, Slow stretch</td><td>No mini-game (floating stays a tap-to-float, as you said).</td></tr>
  </table></div>
  <p class="note">Outside traditions you can play any of them whenever the rec dec is open (06:00 to midnight), alone or with whoever's free.</p>
</section>

<section id="perf">
  <h2>Building it (notes for the rec deck's owner)</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Reuse Lightdisc's kit</b> (section 23b, <code>plans/handover-lightdisc.md</code>): pure rules at a fixed 60 Hz with no <code>R()</code> or <code>Math.random</code>; a clone for checking that a live go matches the go on paper at 60, 30 and 20 fps; the view switch (<code>discViewOn</code>/<code>discFit</code>-style camera, the <code>#ld</code> overlay, <code>body.ld-on</code> hiding the HUD); the ask-and-find flow (<code>discFind</code>, <code>crewComeTo</code>); <code>FullPerson</code> partners with their crowd body hidden; the save pattern (<code>SHIP.disc</code> read defensively). One mini-game module per sport, each with <code>New</code>, <code>Step</code>, <code>Paper</code> and <code>Clone</code>.</li>
    <li><b>Seeds:</b> Lightdisc uses schedU keys 830-832; the three games need about two each. Proposed 833-838, to be checked against the key list first.</li>
    <li><b>Phones:</b> under 200 draw calls in a go (Lightdisc is about 129). Wall holds and gate poles as one merged mesh or an <code>InstancedMesh</code> each; no new lights; the one water material (<code>RK.poolWater</code>); nothing on the water's plane (gates and lane ropes sit above it, the stroke ring is screen overlay).</li>
    <li><b>Poses:</b> new rig poses like <code>discPose</code>: reach and pull, the stroke and the turn, the paddle stroke. You are drawn by the activity's stand-in body, as in Lightdisc.</li>
    <li><b>Checks:</b> a check per game like <code>disc_check.py</code> (rules, save, the frozen clock, draw calls, the frame-rate match), and Decks 1-6 identical in <code>cadet_test.py</code>.</li>
    <li><b>Changes the rec deck plan:</b> its line "Pool, kayaks, wall and float room: a tap plays the animation. No practice, no times" becomes "a tap plays the mini-game; the float room stays a tap".</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section id="q">
  <h2>Questions for the user (with my recommendations)</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><b>The climbing wall:</b> Reach or Bell rush? <i>Recommended: Reach.</i></li>
    <li><b>The pool:</b> Lengths or Ring dive? <i>Recommended: Lengths.</i></li>
    <li><b>The kayaks:</b> White water or Duck rescue? <i>Recommended: White water, with Duck rescue perhaps later as its calm mode.</i></li>
    <li><b>Times and bests:</b> show times, or only bests in words? <i>Recommended: bests in words; real placings only at the regatta, and the bell board.</i></li>
    <li><b>Does the clock stop</b> during a go, as in Lightdisc? <i>Recommended: yes.</i></li>
    <li><b>Crew beside you or racing you?</b> <i>Recommended: beside you at their own pace, and a race only when you ask ("Race me?") or at the regatta.</i></li>
    <li><b>Which first?</b> <i>Recommended: the kayaks (two traditions and Lightdisc's steering), then the wall, then the pool.</i></li>
  </ol>
</section>
